# The font programs this corpus runs, and what each was measured to
# carry. tests/corpus/fetch.sh copies whichever of them the machine
# already has, under the name in the first field; a machine that has
# none leaves the corpus empty and the test skips. The fonts belong to
# their makers and are not kept here.
#
# Four fields: the name the corpus holds it under, whether the font's
# array of procedures runs while the program is loaded, which of those
# procedures its charstrings reach, and what the font is.
#
# Where the middle two come from. Both are properties of the font file
# and of nothing in this tree, read out of the program itself: the
# encrypted section is deciphered with the documented stream cipher, the
# array following /OtherSubrs is read to see whether its first token is
# an operator -- an array whose entries are all procedure bodies is
# built without any of them running, one that opens with a test of its
# own runs as it is built -- and each charstring is deciphered and
# decoded in turn, following the subroutines it calls, for the procedure
# numbers it hands to callothersubr. Number 0 is the one a flex is drawn
# through and number 3 the one a glyph replaces its stem hints through,
# so a font that reaches neither exercises nothing here and is not
# listed.
#
# `runs` is what makes a font material for the question this corpus was
# assembled for. The standard array asks the interpreter for its
# internal dictionary while it is being built, which is the use PLRM 8
# sanctions for that dictionary and the only one it sanctions; an
# `inert` font names the same dictionary inside a procedure body that
# nothing here ever runs. The test wants at least one `runs` font among
# what the corpus holds, and says so rather than passing when it has
# none.

crimson.pfb       runs   flex hint  Crimson roman, Sebastian Kosch
freeeuro.pfa      runs   flex       FreeEuro, the free Euro symbols
charter.pfb       inert  hint       Bitstream Charter roman
nimbusroman.pfb   inert  hint       Nimbus Roman, URW
